      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Execute the rigidity experiment using a synthetic constraint set on a simple function (e.g., $f(x) = \\sin(x)$) to test the hypothesis that rigid solution spaces exhibit $N_c(w) = O(1)$ while fat spaces exhibit $N_c(w) = O(w)$. The plan involves generating constraint pairs $(x_i, y_i)$, training small feedforward networks with varying hidden widths $w$, and measuring the minimal width $N_c$ required to achieve zero violation (max abs error $\\le 1e-3$) on a held-out set. This follows the workflow mode to run the computational pipeline for hypothesis testing."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "For a rigid synthetic function (e.g., linear or low-frequency sinusoidal with few constraints), $N_c(w)$ will plateau at a small constant value (e.g., $N_c \\approx 10-20$) as $w$ increases. For a fat/high-complexity function, $N_c$ will increase linearly or remain unbounded within the tested width range."
    },
